//? Title: Moore DFA minimization
//? Subtitle: Actual synchronous signatures, stable partitions and reachable quotients
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Replace a deterministic finite automaton by a smaller machine recognizing
exactly the same finite words. Moore refinement separates states whose
acceptance or destination classes disagree. We inspect actual synchronous
signatures, retain the original six-state automaton, and distinguish
completed-state classes from the reachable minimal quotient. Native
product-automaton comparison checks language equality for all finite words.
)

\toc

> Derive synchronous distinguishability refinement

Initially group accepting and rejecting states. These classes distinguish the
empty word. At each round, give state q the signature consisting of its old
class followed by the old classes of its destinations in symbol order. Group
equal signatures, assigning new IDs by first state encounter. All signatures use
the same old labels, so the round is synchronous.

$$
\sigma_t(q)=(B_t(q),B_t(\delta(q,0)),\ldots,B_t(\delta(q,k-1))).
$$
\alttext(A state signature includes its old class and the old class reached under each input symbol.)

Induction shows that after t refinements, equal classes agree on acceptance of
every word of length at most t. Including the old class in each signature
prevents merging, so classes only split. When the number of classes stops
growing, the partition is unchanged up to label renaming and is a transition
congruence: induction on arbitrary word length proves language agreement within
each class. Different final classes have a finite distinguishing word inherited
from an earlier split. Removing unreachable quotient classes then gives the
minimal reachable machine.

The native API first validates and completes missing moves or a missing start
with a rejecting sink. It refines all completed states, including unreachable
ones, then numbers the reachable quotient by ascending-symbol breadth-first
search. Internal block IDs and returned state IDs are different numbering
systems.

> A three-state unary chain

For words over symbol0, states0,1,2 count progress toward acceptance at state2,
which loops. The initial labels put0,1 together and2 separately. One refinement
separates0 from1 because their destinations have different old labels. A second
stable pass confirms the partition. The next diagram reads actual block counts
and the table prints every actual produced signature.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/moore_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/dfa_minimization.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t =
        dm::algorithms::moore_minimize_trace(dm::algorithms::moore_chain_dfa(3));
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("initial", {0, 0}, "Actual acceptance partition\n2 blocks");
    std::string previous = "initial";
    std::print("| actual round | state | actual old signature | new block | stable pass |\n| ---: | ---: | " "--- | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.rounds.size(); ++i) {
        auto &r = t.rounds[i];
        for (std::size_t q = 0; q < r.after.size(); ++q)
            std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i + 1, q,
                       r.signatures[q], r.after[q], r.stable);
        auto id = std::to_string(i);
        d.node(id, {6.0 * (i + 1), 0},
               std::format("Actual refinement {}\n{} blocks / stable {}", i + 1,
                           r.blocks_after, r.stable)).edge(previous, id);
        previous = id;
    }
    d.save_svg("build/moore_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual round | state | actual old signature | new block | stable pass |
| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | --- |
| 1 | 0 | [0, 0] | 0 | false |
| 1 | 1 | [0, 1] | 1 | false |
| 1 | 2 | [1, 1] | 2 | false |
| 2 | 0 | [0, 1] | 0 | true |
| 2 | 1 | [1, 2] | 1 | true |
| 2 | 2 | [2, 2] | 2 | true |
\image(figures/moore_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual synchronous signatures and stable-pass count for a unary chain.)
\alttext(The partition grows from2 to3 blocks, then the second native refinement is stable.)

> Retain the original six-state automaton

The original two-symbol DFA has transition
rows(1,2),(0,3),(4,5),(4,5),(4,5),(5,5), start0 and accepting states2,3,4. Its
actual stable classes are{0,1},{2,3,4},{5}, yielding a three-state reachable
quotient. The class diagram below displays native final member lists rather than
drawing a guessed automaton.

The original report compared words only through length five. The native
product-automaton equivalence check now explores every reachable pair: no pair
with different acceptance proves equality for all finite words, while a
differing pair would supply a shortest witness. This strengthens the finite test
without treating a short word sample as a language proof. The actual quotient
transition table is printed separately from internal classes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/moore_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/dfa_minimization.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto source = dm::algorithms::moore_demo_dfa();
    auto t = dm::algorithms::moore_minimize_trace(source);
    auto equivalent = dm::algorithms::dfa_equivalence(source, t.run.result);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    for (std::size_t b = 0; b < t.run.blocks.size(); ++b)
        d.node(std::to_string(b), {6.0 * b, 0},
               std::format("Actual stable class {}\n{}", b, t.run.blocks[b]));
    std::print("Original states: {}. Reachable quotient states: {}. Exact language equality: {}. Product " "pairs examined: {}.\n\n| returned quotient state | accepting byte | symbol0 target | " "symbol1 target |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n",
               source.num_states, t.run.result.num_states,
               equivalent.equivalent, equivalent.pairs_examined);
    for (int q = 0; q < t.run.result.num_states; ++q)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", q, int(t.run.result.accept[q]),
                   t.run.result.delta[q][0], t.run.result.delta[q][1]);
    d.save_svg("build/moore_original.svg");
}
```
