> Completion, missing starts and unreachable classes

A partial accepting one-state machine with a missing transition needs a
rejecting sink and two quotient states. A missing start denotes the empty
language even if explicit states accept; completion adds a rejecting start and
the reachable quotient is one rejecting state. With no symbols, only the empty
word matters. The controls below separate the number of completed states, all
stable classes and returned reachable states, so unreachable refinement work is
not confused with minimal output size.

Projected state and transition caps include any added sink and are checked after
complete validation but before completion copies. Analysis/trace defaults
are100000 states and1000000 transitions, zero disables. The legacy ordinary
function disables these optional policies. Trace units count initial labels plus
each actual round record, before/after labels, signature rows and their
integers: n initially, then1+3n+n*(k+1) per round. Default history cap1000000,
zero disables. Whole-round admission precedes history copies; working maps and
ordinary run output are outside this logical cap, which is not a byte or
peak-memory bound.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/moore_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/dfa_minimization.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    using dm::algorithms::DFA;
    std::vector<DFA> cases{DFA{}, DFA{1, 1, {{-1}}, 0, {1}},
                           DFA{2, 1, {{0}, {1}}, -1, {1, 0}},
                           DFA{3, 0, {{}, {}, {}}, 0, {1, 0, 1}}};
    std::vector<std::string> labels{"empty", "partial", "missing start",
                                    "epsilon only"};
    std::vector<double> completed, classes, result;
    std::print("| input case | actual completed states | actual stable classes | reachable quotient states " "| exact language equality |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < cases.size(); ++i) {
        auto r = dm::algorithms::moore_minimize_run(cases[i]);
        completed.push_back(r.completed.num_states);
        classes.push_back(r.blocks.size());
        result.push_back(r.result.num_states);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", labels[i],
                   r.completed.num_states, r.blocks.size(), r.result.num_states,
                   dm::algorithms::dfa_equivalence(cases[i], r.result).equivalent);
    }
    auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 3);
    for (auto &entry : std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::vector<double>> >{{"completed states", completed}, {"all stable classes", classes}, {"reachable quotient", result}}) {
        auto p =
            dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(entry.second, labels, "actual count");
        p.title(entry.first).x_label("input case").y_label("states or classes").y_limits(0, 4);
        layout.add(p);
    }
    layout.size(1500, 600).fit_panels().save_svg("build/moore_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| input case | actual completed states | actual stable classes | reachable quotient states | exact language equality |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| empty | 1 | 1 | 1 | true |
| partial | 2 | 2 | 2 | true |
| missing start | 3 | 2 | 1 | true |
| epsilon only | 3 | 2 | 1 | true |
\image(figures/moore_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual completion and reachability controls with identical count limits.)
\alttext(Unreachable completed classes can exceed the returned quotient size; exact language comparison remains true for every control.)

> Conclusion and references

Actual synchronous signatures explain every split and stable pass. The original
machine reduces from six states to three, with exact product-language comparison
replacing a bounded word sample. The chain family shows real refinement depth,
while completion and reachability controls separate work over all states from
the minimal reachable output. Numeric block labels are implementation
identities, not semantic state names.

[E. F. Moore (1956), Gedanken-experiments on sequential machines, in Automata Studies|https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400882618-006]
supplies the partition-refinement foundation. The ordered vector-key maps, sink
completion, BFS quotient numbering and capture policy described here specify
this native implementation.

